OPEN
================================================================================
- CLI cannot collect a DATA_ARG_NUMBER arg. smp_groovebox_ui_cli.c's generic
  action-arg collection handles only DATA_ARG_PATH/DATA_ARG_CHOICE and falls
  through to "unsupported arguments". SET_VALUE/ADJUST_VALUE are fully wired
  and correctly gated at the DataOps layer but are unusable from the CLI.
  Needs CLI input work (digit accumulation, decimal point, sign, confirm/
  cancel) - not a DataObject or params.c change.
- CLAP vs the vendored clap/ext/params.h, for item (2):
  * CLAP_PARAM_IS_PERIODIC: no handling, undecided.
  * CLAP_PARAM_IS_BYPASS: empty stub, exposed as an ordinary param.
  * clap_output_events_read never parses a CLAP_EVENT_PARAM_VALUE the plugin
    sends, so plugin-side value changes never reach us.
  * Gesture BEGIN/END never wrapped around SET_VALUE/ADJUST_VALUE.
  * clap_host_params.request_flush()/flush() not audited - matters if a
    plugin is active while process() is not being called.
  * RESCAN_ALL survivor matching is by owner_id against the not-yet-pruned
    container: a plugin that frees a clap_id and reassigns it to an
    unrelated param within the SAME rescan gets the new param misidentified
    as a survivor, discarding its fresh name/min/max/default. Not fixed -
    params.c has no setter for a live param's min/max/inc and adding one
    raises reclamp-in-flight questions. Narrow; revisit if a real plugin
    hits it.
  * Automation/modulation (IS_AUTOMATABLE*/IS_MODULATABLE*, PER_NOTE_ID/
    PER_KEY/PER_CHANNEL/PER_PORT, CLAP_EVENT_PARAM_MOD): out of scope -
    automation needs a timeline engine this app lacks; modulation needs a
    routing concept related to but not reducible to CONNECT.
- Header hygiene: types.h has accumulated defines used by ONE module, which
  forces unrelated recompiles. MAX_CATEGORY_SEGMENT was moved to params.h
  for this reason. Walk the rest: anything referenced from a single .c/.h
  pair belongs in that pair's header; only genuinely cross-cutting things
  (PARAM_T, SAMPLE_T, MAX_SHORT_NAME_LENGTH) stay.

================================================================================
DECISIONS - settled, do not re-litigate
================================================================================
- user_data stays a borrowed POINTER, not a revalidatable key. A key
  ({owner, slot, uid} by value, re-resolved per call) would delete the
  INVARIANT above and make staleness detectable instead of UB - and it is
  already the pattern one layer down (PARAM_RING_DATA_BIT carries val_id AND
  uid for exactly that reason). Deferred anyway: whole-codebase refactor of
  every ops table, every accessor becomes a lookup, and params itself barely
  benefits since val_id stability is load-bearing for the rt side and Trk.
  Revisit only for data owned out of process / freed concurrently, a
  scripting layer defining its own tree, or UI state restored by identity
  across runs (and even then, serialising a path of names beats persisting
  ids).
- Param categories MINT uids; DataChoice category rows HASH their path. Not
  an inconsistency: a DataChoice row is stateless, enumerated on demand with
  no struct to hang identity off. Param categories are real entities with
  storage.
- A param changing category is handled as remove+add and that is CORRECT,
  not a degradation: a param has no children so there is no hover state
  inside it, and the old parent's hover pointing at it SHOULD re-anchor,
  having genuinely left that list. Categories themselves never move under a
  rescan - interning matches by (parent, name), so a changed module path
  interns a new chain and the old entity just goes empty.
- Empty categories are not garbage-collected. The interned entry lingers
  (a pointer + a name buffer, freed with the container); the CX disappears
  because the enumerator stops returning it. Its hover entry goes with it,
  which is correct - a category with no params is genuinely gone.
- TARGET-LIST CONTRACT: ui_layer's only hard obligation is that no stored
  ContextId ever names a DESTROYED context. Beyond that a view may store
  what it likes; a stale-but-valid id self-heals (helper_nav_context_single_
  purpose_set scans the parent's live children and falls back to index 0).
  What makes that safe is DataOps.id's "never reused for a different object".
  UiStalePolicy modes are ergonomics, not safety.
- Ports are NOT CX. They churn on every plugin instantiate, and each one
  costs a CX alloc + hash insert + event + a reconcile pass over every view's
  target lists. The two things a CX would buy - resync and navigation - are
  already had from the cache plus the windowed traversal. Revisit ONLY for a
  browse-style patchbay where a CONNECTION is a navigable object with its own
  actions; that, not the churn, is the decision point. A grid view is not a
  tree and would not fit either way.
- Port identity is MINTED, not hashed. jack_funcs interns every port name it
  has seen; the key is that slot + 1 (so 0 stays "no port"), and the table
  outlives the port list - a key survives a rebuild, and a port that
  disappears and comes back keeps it. A rename re-keys the ident in place,
  because jack keeps the port and its connections across a rename so the
  identity must too. Renaming onto an already-interned name takes that
  identity instead.
- jack_funcs does not know what owns a port. app_jack_create_port_on_client
  takes owner_tag + owner_uid: two opaque numbers it stores and hands back on
  JackPortInfo, interpreting neither. app_data passes (namespace, local), so
  MAKE_ID(tag, uid) reconstructs the owner's real ContextId with no mapping
  table. Sampler and main I/O supply both numbers, being one owner each;
  plugin hosts supply only the tag, their instances minting their own uids.
- Port list filtering is UI-SIDE, on a DataChoice.group_key the data layer
  publishes as a FIELD. Never parse a group out of a label - that hardcodes a
  display format in the consumer. Lists stay dense so the window traversal
  never needs a second index space. "Client" is the wrong axis: every port
  this app registers lives on one jack client, so grouping by client
  collapses every plugin into a single row.
- app_jack_ports_sync is the ONLY consumer of the ports/connections dirty
  flags - they are read-and-clear, so a second reader silently eats the
  notification. Our own create/unregister/rename set ports_changed directly
  rather than waiting on jack's notification thread; the callbacks stay
  trivial because the cache tables are main-thread data.
- is_hidden/value_as_string/value_range stay their own DataOps entries
  rather than generic "properties": they are read on hot paths or gate
  behaviour. DATA_CAP_PROPERTIES was built for the param category and
  removed with it - it had no consumer.
